title: Trash, undo and redo
tags: Trash
kind: reference
summary: A trashing is an ordinary revision: undo brings the file back, redo sends it back, whichever way the log is walked.

A trashing writes its own revision, stamped `origin = 'trash'`. It is not the
watcher's (only `watcher` revisions are out of an undo's reach), so it can be
undone like any change you made.

!! Two ways back

* ''Restore the entry'' (`mf trash restore`, the [[trash panel]]): the client recreates the metarecords from the capture and moves the bytes back ([[What restoring does]]).
* ''Undo, rollback, revert'': the daemon recreates the metarecords, from the log's own inverse of the deletion; the client moves the bytes back from the trash and consumes the entry.

Each side stays in its own domain — the daemon never touches a file, the client
never recreates a metarecord behind the daemon's back — and both end the same
way: the same metarecords at the same uuids, the bytes at the original path.

The capture in the entry is not redundant with the log: the log can be pruned
(`mf log prune`), and the trash promises durability. Once a trashing is pruned
out of the log there is no inverse left to apply, and restoring the entry is the
only way back.

!! The daemon says what to do on disk

An ordinary deletion of a metarecord touches no file, so the op type cannot tell
the client that bytes are involved. The daemon does: every step of a navigation,
and every operation of a revert plan, carries a `filesystem` action
(spec-event-log "Coordinated navigation"):

* `restore_content` — a metarecord a trashing took is coming back: the client finds the live entry holding it and moves its bytes back;
* `trash_content` — it is going again (a redo): the client sends the file back to the trash as a trashing does, captured first, into a new entry.

Both hold whichever way the redo goes: HEAD forward over the trashing, a rollback
over the revert an undo wrote, or a revert of that revert (the watcher having
written in between). Those last two are ordinary-looking creations and deletions;
the daemon follows their `reverts_op_id` chain back to the trashing.

The entry is found by the ''metarecord'' it holds, never by an id recorded on
the operation: an undo consumes the entry and a redo makes a new one, so a
recorded id would name an entry that no longer exists. There can never be two
live entries for one metarecord. When none holds it — the trash was pruned — the
metadata rewinds and the bytes stay gone, with a note saying so: a rollback is
never blocked by content you chose to discard.

A redo that sent only the metadata forward would delete the metarecords and leave
the file on disk, where the watcher would never see it again: that was a bug
until September 2026.

The same code does this for `mf log undo`/`redo`/`rollback`/`revert` and for the
GUI's `log:undo`, `log:redo`, `log:rollback` and `log:revert`
(`metafolder_core::navigation`).

!! A rollback that would overwrite a file

When a rollback moves a file back onto a path something else now occupies, the
occupant goes to the trash first (reason `rollback`, no metarecord captured: the
step names the moved file, not the occupant). A ''directory'' in the way is not
trashed: the step is skipped, the file stays where it is, and a note says so.
